Evaluating arguments | Evaluate claims by checking evidence, assumptions, and missing perspectives. A strong counterpoint addresses reasoning rather than attacking the writer. | “Every student prefers online books” is unsupported by a survey of one small club. | Explain why the sample does not justify the word every. | The club is a limited group and may not represent all students. | A school website posts two opinions on digital textbooks. | Write a paragraph comparing each claim and one limitation. | The paragraph names evidence for both sides and avoids false certainty.
Academic summaries | A summary states the main purpose, methods, key points, and limits in fewer words without adding new claims. Paraphrased ideas still need source attribution. | A report of twenty volunteers cannot become a claim about all teenagers in the summary. | Write a cautious one-sentence summary of a small volunteer survey. | Example: “Among twenty volunteers, most preferred the earlier time; the result may not represent all students.” | Your class prepares a one-page English summary of a local study. | Outline question, method, findings, and limitation with a source line. | Each point matches the study, and uncertainty remains visible.
Formal correspondence | Professional messages state purpose, relevant background, requested action, and deadline politely. Avoid sharing sensitive information before verifying the recipient. | An email asking for workshop details should name the event and question, not send copies of identity documents without need. | Draft a subject line and opening for a workshop inquiry. | “Inquiry about the reading workshop” and a polite request clearly identify the purpose. | A student asks a university club about a public online seminar. | Write a concise email with two questions and a closing. | The recipient can answer without requesting unnecessary personal data.
Interviews and listening | Interviews use open questions, follow-up prompts, and careful listening. Notes should distinguish the speaker's words from the interviewer's interpretation; obtain consent before recording. | “What helped you learn?” invites explanation more than “Was it easy?” | Change a yes/no question about a project into an open question. | Example: “Which part of the project was most challenging, and why?” | A group interviews a librarian about services for new students. | Write three open questions and a plan for confirming notes. | Ask permission, avoid private patron data, and let the librarian review factual notes if possible.
Persuasive presentations | A persuasive presentation links an achievable proposal to evidence and audience concerns. The conclusion asks for a specific action without overstating benefits. | A proposal for an extra study table should show demand, space, cost, and how success will be checked. | State one specific request and one piece of evidence for that proposal. | Request a trial table for one month, supported by documented busy periods. | A team pitches a low-cost reading activity to school leaders. | Write an opening claim, two reasons, response to one concern, and closing ask. | The pitch is feasible, transparent about limits, and respectful of decision makers.
Literature and cultural context | Literary works reflect choices of language, form, and cultural context. Readers should avoid treating one character as representative of an entire community. | A character's action in a story is evidence about that character, not proof all people from the same place act alike. | Explain why a single fictional character cannot stand for a whole culture. | Fiction selects particular events and viewpoints; communities contain many people and experiences. | Read an original short story from a different context with a reliable note on its setting. | Write a response linking one detail to context and one question needing more information. | The response cites text and source, while marking uncertain cultural interpretations.
